# Controllers design

Controllers contains the Fearless manager: a desktop program that keeps a set of project folders, compiles those projects, runs the mains of those projects, and connects those projects to the desk and to Eclipse.
Next to the manager, and independent from the manager, Controllers also contains:
- `fearlessPluginProject`: the Eclipse plugin (see EclipseDesign).
- `agentTools`: a library driving the real pointer and keyboard, for GUI tests of any program.
- `benchmarks`: speed measures of Fearless programs against the runtime.

The manager is an island: the manager behaves the same on every desk.
When a part of the desk (a tray, a dialog, a desktop shell) would make the manager behave differently than this design, the manager does not use that part that way.
When the desk does not offer what the manager needs (a display to the manager process that becomes the 'single' manager, a file system feature the manager relies on, at most one identity of the Fearless family on the desk (see Asking the user in ReconciliatorDesign), or each Fearless extension (see File associations)), or the manager folder exists and does not contain what the manager wrote there, the manager ends with a 'fatal error' saying so.

A 'fatal error' ends the manager, in this order:
-A dialog shows the error and lists the 'mains chains' and the compilations that are running. Nothing below happens until the dialog has been closed.
-Every running 'mains chain' and compilation is ended. The manager waits until every 'mains chain' and compilation has ended.
-The manager deletes the manager folder.
-The manager removes the setup of every identity of the Fearless family (`.eradicateAll`, see ReconciliatorDesign).

Everything the manager is asked to do either happens or is refused with an error naming what was refused and the reason of the refusal.

The algorithms of this design specify the behaviour, not the implementation: an implementation may take the steps of an algorithm in another order, or in another way, when no person and no program can tell the difference, also after a crash.

## Program and manager folder

The program folder of a manager is `fearlessManaged<version>`, for example `fearlessManaged0_001`.
On macOS the program folder is the application `fearlessManaged<version>.app`.
The 'launcher' of the manager is the executable file of the program folder that started the manager program; the program folder contains two launchers, with the identities `<i>` and `<i>w` in the Fearless family (see The data in ReconciliatorDesign).
The manager folder of a manager is `fearless<version>`, next to the program folder, on every desk. The manager folder contains everything the manager remembers; deleting the manager folder makes the manager start from nothing.
A manager folder that can not be created is a 'fatal error' naming the manager folder.

## Starting the 'single' manager
A 'manager process' is an OS process running the manager program.
A manager process is 'fresh' when it starts.
A 'fresh' manager process becomes a 'single candidate' when it takes the lock of the `instance.lock` of its manager folder (see The 'single' manager in Implementation notes).
A 'single candidate' becomes the 'single' manager when its first `.reconcile` (see Reconciling) gives up no Fearless extension.
A manager process never goes back to an earlier state.
Only the 'single' manager handles the messages, except `Quit` (see `replaced` in File associations).

The manager program takes:
- zero arguments: the message is `Show`;
- one argument starting with `FearlessCommand<Verb>`, a `call` (see Requests and messages): the message is the verb `<Verb>` with the arguments of the verb and the `port` that follow in the `call`;
- one argument not starting with `FearlessCommand`: the path of a file or a folder, as the desk passes the path when the file is opened (for example by a double click); the messages are `Register` with that path, then `Show`, for a folder or a `.fearless` file, and the message is `OpenWithOrShortcut` with that path otherwise.
For example:
```
"FearlessCommandQuit"
"FearlessCommandShow hello_world"
"FearlessCommandSelectMains hello_world hello.Hello1 hello.Hello3"
"FearlessCommandRun hello_world Port:51234"
"FearlessCommandRegister Base16:2F646174612F68656C6C6F"
```
(the last one registers `/data/hello` on Linux).
macOS passes an opened file to a manager process as an 'event', not as an argument.
The script of the 'helper bundle' of the manager (see Extensions on macOS in ReconciliatorDesign) starts the manager with the one argument `FearlessCommandOpened`.
On macOS the one argument `FearlessCommandOpened` writes no message; on every other desk `FearlessCommandOpened` is a bad argument.
The messages of an event are the messages of one argument not starting with `FearlessCommand`, for each path of the event.
On macOS the 'single' manager writes the messages of every event.
On macOS a 'fresh' manager process that can not take the lock, started with zero arguments or with `FearlessCommandOpened`, waits up to 2 seconds for one event after writing its messages, then writes the messages of that event.
A manager process that becomes the 'single' manager applies its own messages like every other message.
A manager process started with two or more arguments, or with a bad argument, writes the message `InvalidCall` with the error naming the first bad argument, or saying that the manager program takes at most one argument. When the 'single' manager handles `InvalidCall`, the 'single' manager shows the error in a dialog and continues running.
```
A No manager process is running.
B The user starts the manager program.
C The manager process encounters a 'fatal error'.
D The manager process becomes a 'single candidate'.
E The manager process ends with the 'fatal error'.

A The 'single' manager is running.
B The user starts the manager program again.
C The new manager process encounters a 'fatal error'.
D The new manager process writes the message FatalError with the error.
E The new manager process ends with exit status 0.
F The 'single' manager ends with that 'fatal error'.

A No manager process is running.
B A script, in a session with no display, runs the manager program with FearlessCommandRun hello.
C The manager process becomes a 'single candidate'.
D The manager process ends with a 'fatal error': no display.
E The error goes to the standard error.

A The 'single' manager is running.
B A script, in a session with no display, runs the manager program with FearlessCommandRun hello.
C The manager process writes the message Run hello.
D The manager process ends.
E The 'single' manager runs project hello.
```
```
A The user runs the manager program without the launcher of a manager.
B The manager process can not know its manager folder.
C The manager process shows the error.
D The manager process ends.
E Unlike a 'fatal error', the manager process deletes no folder, writes no message, and removes no setup: without the launcher, the manager process knows neither its manager folder nor its identity.
```

The 'single' manager runs until `Quit`, `Reset`, a 'fatal error', or the deletion of the manager folder (see I3 in File associations).

## Requests and messages

A 'request' is what one of the verbs below asks the manager to do, whether the request comes from a message or from a GUI operation.
The 'single' manager applies the requests one at a time, in the order they arrive, each one completely before the next, except a `Quit` message (see `replaced` in File associations).
Applying a `Run` or a `Compile` is starting the 'mains chain' or the compilation; applying a `Terminate` ends once the 'mains chain' or the compilation has ended.

A 'message' is the file `messages/<time>-<id>.msg` in the manager folder: `<time>` is the 20 digit count of milliseconds since 1970 when the message was written, and `<id>` is a random UUID. The 'single' manager applies the messages in the order of their names, and deletes each message after reading it.
Only the manager program writes messages: the manager program writes `messages/<time>-<id>.tmp`, then renames `messages/<time>-<id>.tmp` to `messages/<time>-<id>.msg`, so the 'single' manager never reads a partial message. The 'single' manager deletes every `.tmp` file older than 60 seconds.
A manager process whose `.tmp` or `.msg` name already exists picks another `<id>`; the rename never replaces an existing file.
The 'single' manager learns of a new message from the report of the desk that a file appeared in `messages`. When a manager process becomes the 'single' manager, the 'single' manager writes a `.tmp` file in `messages` and waits for the report of that `.tmp` file; no report within 5 seconds is a 'fatal error'.
Every other program commanding the manager (the Eclipse plugin, a script, a test) runs the manager program with a `call` as its argument.
Every GUI operation that is a request has an equivalent message, and every verb is the verb of a GUI operation.
Docs, Base documentation, Browse, Logs and Fearless demos, which open another program on a file or a web page, Info, which opens the Info dialog, the Clear button of an Output, which empties the Output, and Import zip, which writes files and then sends requests, are GUI operations that are not requests and have no message. A GUI operation that finds nothing to open says so in a dialog.
The error of an ill formed message, except a message read while the manager is quitting, shows in a dialog of the manager window.
Showing the manager window asks the desk to bring the manager window to the front.

A message contains a verb, then the arguments of the verb, then the `port` when the message comes from a `call` with a `port`, one per line.
A verb is one of the following, written in upper camel case. Verbs about the whole manager:
- `Show`: show the manager window; with the name of a registered project as its argument, also select that project;
- `Register <path>`: register the folder, or the folder a file is in, then select that project; a folder 'registered already' is only selected;
- `Quit`: end the manager and every 'mains chain' and compilation the manager runs;
- `Reset`: end the manager as a 'fatal error' ends the manager (the steps at the top of this document), with no dialog;
- `ConnectEclipse <path>`: connect the Eclipse found from the path, then say so in a dialog, asking to restart Eclipse if Eclipse is running and to open the Fearless perspective;
- `EditMetadata <path>`: replace `projects.info` with the content of the file at the path;
- `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>`: open the file (see Opening a file). Double clicking the file on the desktop is the GUI operation of `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>`.
- `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`: open the file with the main `<main>` of the project `<name>`, one of the mains claiming the extension of the file. Picking a main in the conflict dialog is the GUI operation of `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`.
Verbs about one project, taking the name of a registered project:
- `Run <name>`: select the project and run the selected mains of the project as a 'mains chain', in the order of the selected mains;
- `Compile <name>`: select the project and compile the project if the project is 'out of date'; otherwise the Output says that the project was already up to date;
- `Check <name>`: select the project and report in the Output of the project the 'problem' of the project, or that the project is 'out of date', or that the project is neither 'invalid' nor 'out of date';
- `Terminate <name>`: select the project and end the 'mains chain' or the compilation of the project, if any;
- `DeleteCache <name>`: select the project and delete the compilation cache of the project;
- `Kind <name> <kind>`: set the kind of the project to `<kind>`;
- `Forget <name>`: forget the project;
- `Fix <name>`: select the project and fix the project folder (see Fix in FolderShapesDesign);
- `Mount <name> <type> <data>`: set the mount of the DataRead type `<type>` of the project to the data project `<data>` (see DataReadDataEditDesign);
- `Unmount <name> <type>`: remove the mount of the DataRead type `<type>` of the project;
- `Attenuate <name> <main> <attenuator1>..<attenuatorn>`: set the attenuators of the main `<main>` of the project to the attenuators `<attenuator1>..<attenuatorn>`, applied in order; no `<attenuator>` removes all the attenuators of `<main>` (see Manager in AttenuationDesign);
- `SelectMains <name> <main1>..<mainn>`: set the selected mains of the project to the mains given; no `<main>` removes the selected mains;
`Forget`, `Kind`, `Mount`, `Unmount`, `Attenuate` and `SelectMains` are special cases of `EditMetadata`: each one is `EditMetadata` with the current `projects.info` changed in one place (the project removed, the `kind` of the project replaced, the mount of one DataRead type replaced or removed, the attenuators of one main replaced, the selected mains of the project replaced).
Such a verb is applied, or is ill formed with the same error, exactly as that `EditMetadata` is.
Before applying the request of a `call` with a `port`, the manager connects to that port of this machine.
When the connection is made, what the manager sends on the connection starts with a line saying what became of the message: `Applied` once the request is applied, or `Refused` once the message is found ill formed.
After `Refused`, the manager sends the error, as the dialog shows the error, and ends the connection.
After `Applied`, the manager ends the connection at once, except for `Run`, `Compile`, `Check` and `Fix`.
For `Run`, `Compile`, `Check` and `Fix`, after `Applied`, the manager also sends on the connection what the request writes in the Output, from the start of the request and then as the request writes it, with the marks of the Output (see Run, Compile and Check).
The connection then ends when the 'mains chain' or the compilation ends; when no 'mains chain' and no compilation starts, the connection ends once the text the request writes in the Output is sent.
`Quit` and `Reset` may end the manager before the manager sends `Applied`.
Two more kinds of message, written only by a manager process about a failure of that manager process, contain `InvalidCall` or `FatalError` on the first line and the error, as a dialog shows the error, on the lines after the first line. `InvalidCall` and `FatalError` are not verbs: no `call` and no GUI operation names those messages.

```
call ::= (FearlessCommandShow projectName?
  | FearlessCommandQuit
  | FearlessCommandReset
  | FearlessCommandRegister base16
  | FearlessCommandConnectEclipse base16
  | FearlessCommandEditMetadata base16
  | FearlessCommandOpenWithOrShortcut base16
  | FearlessCommandOpenWithOrShortcut base16 projectName typeName
  | FearlessCommandRun projectName
  | FearlessCommandCompile projectName
  | FearlessCommandCheck projectName
  | FearlessCommandTerminate projectName
  | FearlessCommandDeleteCache projectName
  | FearlessCommandKind projectName kind
  | FearlessCommandForget projectName
  | FearlessCommandFix projectName
  | FearlessCommandMount projectName typeName projectName
  | FearlessCommandUnmount projectName typeName
  | FearlessCommandAttenuate projectName typeName typeName1..typeNamen
  | FearlessCommandSelectMains projectName typeName1..typeNamen) port?
kind ::= idle | code | dataRead | dataEdit
port ::= Port:1 .. Port:65535
projectName ::= a 'valid segment' (see Names in FolderShapesDesign)
typeName ::= (?!(con|prn|aux|nul|com[1-9]|lpt[1-9])[.])[a-z][a-z0-9_]*[.]_*[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*'*
```
The `typeName1..typeNamen` of `FearlessCommandSelectMains` are all different.
The `base16` of a `call` names an absolute path that is not the root of a drive.
An argument starting with `FearlessCommand` is a 'bad argument' when the argument is not a `call`, except `FearlessCommandOpened` on macOS (see Starting the 'single' manager).

Ill formed messages:
- a port where no program of this machine accepts the connection;
- every message the manager reads from the start of a `Quit` or a `Reset` until the manager ends, with an error naming the verb of the message and saying that the manager is quitting;
- a `projectName` that is not the name of a registered project; the error lists the registered projects, except for the `<name>` of `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`, whose error lists the mains claiming the extension of the file (see the `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>` entry below);
- a `Register` whose folder (the path, or the folder containing the file of the path) is not 'registered already' and, added at the end of `projects.info`, is a project folder the manager can not reach (see `reach` in Registered projects);
- a `ConnectEclipse` whose path leads to no 'Eclipse folder' (see EclipseDesign), and an `EditMetadata` whose path names no file;
- an `OpenWithOrShortcut` whose path names no file or names an empty file;
- an `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>` where `<main>` of `<name>` is not one of the mains claiming the extension of the file, also when `<name>` is not a registered project (the error lists the mains claiming the extension);
- an `EditMetadata` that Registered projects refuses;
- `Run` and `Compile` of a project that is not code, naming the kind of the project;
- `Run` of a project whose `attenuators` name a main that is not a main of the project, or an attenuator that is not an attenuator of the project (the error lists the mains, or the attenuators);
- `Run` of a project with a broken mount, or with a DataRead type with no mount (see DataReadDataEditDesign; the error lists each broken mount, naming what makes that mount broken, and each DataRead type with no mount);
- `Run` and `Compile` of an 'invalid' project (the error is the 'problem' of the project);
- `Run` of a project that is 'out of date', naming that state; `Run` of a project with no selected main, or with a selected main that is not a main of the project (the error lists the mains of the project);
- a request about a 'busy' project (see Run, Compile and Check), other than `Show` and `Terminate`; an `EditMetadata` is about each project the edit removes, changes an entry of, or deletes the compilation cache of, and about each project with a mount that the edit makes name another project folder;
- an `OpenWithOrShortcut` that runs a main of a project where a `Run` of only that main is ill formed (the error is the error of that `Run`).
```
A Project a has the folder /data/a.
B The user asks to register the folder /data/a/sub.
C The manager refuses: /data/a/sub is inside the folder of project a.
```

Whether a message is ill formed is decided when the 'single' manager starts applying the request of the message, after the 'autoCheck' (see Project states) of the project the request runs on, from the message and the state that 'autoCheck' finds, before anything is compiled, run or written to the Output.
The error of an ill formed message is never in the Output: the Output of a project contains only the results of well formed requests. A failing compilation is the result of a well formed request.

## Info files

The files the manager writes for the manager and for Eclipse (`projects.info`, `manager.info`) are Info files: text in the Fearless character set (the characters a Fearless `Str` allows).
```
info ::= ws (string | list | object) ws
list ::= [[] ws []] | [[] info ([,] info)* []]
object ::= [{] ws [}] | [{] member ([,] member)* [}]
member ::= ws string ws [:] info
string ::= ["](strChar | [\\]["\\n])*["]
ws ::= [ \n]*
```
A `strChar` is a character of the Fearless character set other than `"` and `\`.
The keys of one object are distinct strings.
Inside a string, `\"` is a quote, `\\` a backslash and `\n` a newline.

A path in an Info file is a `taggedText`:
```
taggedText ::= Str:str | UStr:uStr | base16
base16 ::= Base16:([0-9A-F][0-9A-F])+
str ::= strAtom strMore1..strMoren [ ]*
strMore ::= [ ]*[+] strAtom | [ ]*[|^] strAtom?
strAtom ::= [ ]*"[^"\n]*" | [ ]*`[^`\n]*` | [ ]*[(] str [)]
uStr ::= uStrAtom uStrMore1..uStrMoren [ ]*
uStrMore ::= [ ]*[+] uStrAtom | [ ]*[|^] uStrAtom? | [ ]*[.]u codePoints
codePoints ::= [ ]*["]codePoint([ ]codePoint)*["] | [ ]*[`]codePoint([ ]codePoint)*[`]
codePoint ::= [0-9A-F]{1,6}
uStrAtom ::= strAtom [.]u codePoints? | [ ]*[(] uStr [)]
```
Parentheses nest at most 100 deep.
Each `codePoint` is at most `10FFFF` and not from `D800` to `DFFF`.
The text of `Str:str` is the `Str` the Fearless expression `str` evaluates to, and the text of `UStr:uStr` is the `UStr` the Fearless expression `uStr` evaluates to (see `Str` and `UStr` in the standard library: a literal contains its characters as they are, so `\n` inside a literal is two characters).
The text of `base16` is the path whose bytes are the hexadecimal pairs of `base16` (see The bytes of a `Base16:` path in Implementation notes).
For example:
- `"C:\data\hello"` is the path `C:\data\hello`, written in an Info file as `"path": "Str:\"C:\\data\\hello\""`;
- `"Hello"|"World"` is `Hello\nWorld`, encoded as `"Str:\"Hello\"|\"World\""`;
- `"caf".u"00E9"` is `caf` followed by the character `00E9`, encoded as `"UStr:\"caf\".u\"00E9\""`.
The manager writes `Str:` then `.escape` of the path when the path is a `Str`; `UStr:` then `.escape` of the path when the path is a `UStr` and not a `Str`; the `base16` of the path when the path is neither a `Str` nor a `UStr`.

## Registered projects

The manager folder contains `projects.info`, the record of the registered projects and of the 'reserve' (see File associations).
The manager is the only program writing `projects.info`, and 'replaces' `projects.info` (see The Coordinator in CoordinatorDesign).

`projects.info` is an Info file: an object with `projects`, the list of the registered projects, and `reserve`:
```
{
  "projects": [
    {
      "path": "Str:\"C:\\data\\01_hello_world\"",
      "kind": "code",
      "mains": ["hello.Hello1", "hello.Hello3"],
      "mounts": {"hello.Diary": "diary"},
      "attenuators": {"hello.Hello1": ["base.NoManagerQuit", "hello.NoGui"]}
    },
    {
      "path": "UStr:\"C:\\Users\\Jos\".u\"00E9\"+(\"\\da\".u\"1F600\")+(\"ta\".u)",
      "kind": "dataEdit"
    }
  ],
  "reserve": ["htm"]
}
```
- `reserve`: the extensions of the 'reserve' (see File associations), each one a system extension (see File associations) without the dot, with 1 to 16 characters, each `a-z` or `0-9`, listed once.
`projects.info` has no member other than `projects` and `reserve`.
Each project has `path` and `kind`, may have `mains`, `mounts` and `attenuators`, and has no other member; an absent member is the empty list or the empty object:
- `path`: the absolute path of the project folder, as a `taggedText`; no two projects have `path`s with equal texts (see Info files).
The manager can reach the project folder of the project number i of `projects.info` when the `path` of that project corresponds to a p_i (see Desks and Implementation notes in FolderShapesDesign) with `reach(D, i)`, for m the manager folder:
```
reach(D, i)  iff  at(D, p_i)= id, not root(D, p_i), not same(D, p_i, m), not inside(D, p_i, m), not inside(D, m, p_i),
                  forall j < i with reach(D, j): not same(D, p_i, p_j), not inside(D, p_i, p_j), not inside(D, p_j, p_i),
                  and the desk refuses no create(D, p_i s, file) that a same or an inside above with first argument p_i needs
```
- `kind`: `idle`, `code`, `dataRead` or `dataEdit`.
- `mains`: the selected mains, set by `SelectMains`: distinct `typeName`s (see the grammar in Requests and messages).
- `mounts`: for each DataRead type, a `typeName`, the name of the data project mounted on that DataRead type, a `projectName` (see DataReadDataEditDesign).
- `attenuators`: for each main, a `typeName`, the attenuators the manager applies to that main, in order, each a `typeName`, an attenuator possibly more than once; a main absent from `attenuators` gets no attenuator. Naming a main or an attenuator the project does not have is well formed.

`projects.info` contains no project name (see Registering a folder).
The 'single' manager keeps the names in memory.
A 'name lookup' gives a name to every project of `projects.info`, in the order of `projects.info`: name inference (see Registering a folder) names each project.
A 'name check' of a project compares the name of the first marker of the project folder (none when the project folder contains no marker) with the name in memory; when the two names differ, the manager does a 'name lookup'.
The manager does a 'name lookup' at start; before applying a request, the manager does a 'name check' of every project the request names, and a 'name lookup' when the request names a name that is not the name of a project; an 'autoCheck' of a project starts with a 'name check' of the project.
A project folder the manager can not reach keeps the name the manager knew, and gets no name at start (see the 'unreachable dialog' in Project states).

```
A (greet.Hello) in hello.mains.
B (base.NoManagerQuit) in hello.greet.Hello.attenuators.
C (greet.Diary->diary) in hello.mounts.
D The user makes project hello an idle project.
E The user makes project hello a code project.
F The user compiles project hello.
G (greet.Hello) in hello.mains, (base.NoManagerQuit) in hello.greet.Hello.attenuators and (greet.Diary->diary) in hello.mounts, as before.

A (greet.Diary->diary) in hello.mounts.
B Project diary is a dataEdit project with the folder real_diary.
C The user forgets project diary.
D The user registers the folder test_diary, containing the marker diary.fearless.
E The registered project is named diary, and is idle.
F The mount of greet.Diary is broken.
G The user makes project diary a dataEdit project.
H The user runs project hello.
I greet.Hello reads and writes the files of test_diary.
```

A `projects.info` that is not a valid Info file, or does not contain what is above other than the conditions on project folders, is a 'fatal error' pointing at the offending text in the style of the errors of the Frontend.

The whole `projects.info` can be edited by hand from the GUI, or via an `EditMetadata` naming a file (outside of the manager folder) with the intended new content.
The editor of the GUI remembers the content the editor opened; when the user commits, and `projects.info` does not contain that content, the editor refuses the commit in a dialog, and the user edits the new content again; otherwise the commit is an `EditMetadata`.
A project of the edit is a project of `projects.info` when the texts of the two `path`s are equal.
An edit is refused, and nothing changes, when:
- the text, as `projects.info`, would be a 'fatal error' (above): the error is the error of that 'fatal error';
- the edit adds a project whose project folder the manager can not reach;
- the edit is about a 'busy' project (see Requests and messages).
An accepted edit applies at once: projects the edit removes are forgotten, projects the edit adds are registered as they are, the compilation cache of each added project, of each project whose kind changes and of each project whose mains claim an extension the edit removes from `reserve` is deleted (`Coordinator.deleteCache`), and every project gets an 'autoCheck'.

## Registering a folder

A 'marker' of a folder is a file `<name>.fearless` at the top of that folder, with `<name>` not starting with `.`.

A folder p with `same(D, p, p2)` for the project folder p2 of a registered project is 'registered already'.
Registering a folder that is not 'registered already' adds the project at the end of `projects.info`, named by name inference.
'Name inference' gives the project number i of `projects.info` the name name(i), with m the `<name>` of the first marker of the project folder in the order of the s (see Implementation notes in FolderShapesDesign; no m when the project folder contains no marker), and f the first 64 characters of the 'fixed segment' (see FolderShapesDesign) of the name of the project folder:
```
name(i) = m                                            when ok(i, m)
        = the first n of f, f2, f3, ... with ok(i, n)  otherwise
ok(i, n)  iff  n is a 'valid segment', n != name(j) for every j < i,
               and the project folder contains no file other than the first marker named n.fearless, ignoring case
```
f2, f3, ... are f followed by `2`, `3`, ....
The first marker is renamed (or the marker is created) to match; a created marker, and an empty first marker, then contain one newline.
The 'compilation cache' of a project is the folder `.fearless_out` of the project folder; the compilation cache belongs to the Coordinator, whatever the kind of the project.
If present, the compilation cache of the newly registered project is deleted (`Coordinator.deleteCache`).
The newly registered project is in the `idle` state.

Unlike a delete in the file explorer, forgetting a project changes nothing in the project folder; forgetting asks no confirmation.

## Project states

Each project is in the first of these states that holds, shown as a badge on the tile of the project:
- `<kind>: busy`: the project is 'busy';
- `<kind>: invalid content`: the project folder is not 'well shaped' (see FolderShapesDesign; for code, also the checks of `Coordinator.check`, see CoordinatorDesign), the project folder contains more than one marker, or the folder `.config/icon` contains more than one PNG file or a PNG file that can not be read;
- `idle`;
- `dataRead`, `dataEdit`;
- `code: compilation cache out of date`: the project is 'out of date';
- `code: compiled`: the project is neither 'invalid' nor 'out of date'.

A project of kind code, dataRead or dataEdit is 'invalid' when a cause of the state `<kind>: invalid content` holds, also in the state `<kind>: busy`. The 'problem' of an 'invalid' project is the error naming one of the causes above that hold.
An 'autoCheck' of a project reads the project folder and finds the state of the project and the mains of the project.
An 'autoCheck' that finds the project 'invalid' while no compilation of the project runs writes the 'problem' into `.fearless_out/_error.txt` with `Coordinator.writeError`, for every kind of project.
An 'autoCheck' that can not reach the project folder also ends the compilation of the project, if running, and shows the 'unreachable dialog' of the project: an error naming the project folder, with the buttons Locate and Forget.
Closing the 'unreachable dialog' is a `Forget` of the project.
Locate opens a folder picker, then sends an `EditMetadata` giving the picked folder as the `path` of the project; a refused `EditMetadata` shows its error, then the 'unreachable dialog' again.
```
A Code project hello has the folder /home/user/hello, and (greet.Diary->diary) in hello.mounts.
B The user moves /home/user/hello to /home/user/old/hello.
C An 'autoCheck' of project hello can not reach /home/user/hello.
D The manager shows the 'unreachable dialog' of project hello.
E The user presses Locate.
F The user picks /home/user/old/hello.
G Project hello has the folder /home/user/old/hello, and (greet.Diary->diary) in hello.mounts.

A The folders of the projects hello and world are unreachable.
B The manager shows the 'unreachable dialog' of project hello.
C The user presses Forget.
D The manager forgets project hello.
E The manager shows the 'unreachable dialog' of project world.
```

An 'autoCheck' of a code project checks the marker and the icon of the project, then calls `Coordinator.check` (see CoordinatorDesign), which gives the state of the project: 'invalid' with the 'problem', 'out of date' (`State.outOfDate`), or `State.compiled`.

The mains of a code project are known when `Coordinator.check` gives `State.compiled`: the `.mains` of the `.manifest` of `State.compiled`.

Showing an error shows the manager window.
While the manager window is shown and not minimized, the manager notices changes made on disk by other programs: every three seconds the manager runs an 'autoCheck' of the selected project and of one other project, taking the other projects in turn. A project gets an 'autoCheck' when a main or a compilation of the project ends.
The 'autoCheck' every three seconds may keep the outcome of the previous 'autoCheck' of the project when every file and every folder of the project folder has a last modification time before the start of that previous 'autoCheck'.
A request (`Check`, `Compile`, `Run`, `Fix`, `OpenWithOrShortcut`) reads the files of the project from scratch, using no outcome of an earlier 'autoCheck'.

The icon of a project is the only PNG file in the folder `.config/icon` of the project, or, with no PNG file in `.config/icon`, an icon generated from the project name.
A claim whose icon file can not be read shows, in the manager window, the icon generated from `project::pkg.Main`.

## General GUI shape
Closing the manager window hides the manager window.
```
A The 'single' manager is running and shows the manager window.
B The user closes the manager window.
C The user, following the conventions of the desk, is easily able to see that the 'single' manager is still running.
```
On macOS the manager shows this with the Dock icon of the manager, and shows no tray icon.
Clicking the Dock icon is `Show`.
The Quit of macOS (in the menu of the Dock icon, in the application menu, and Cmd+Q) is `Quit`.
On any other desk with a tray (a system tray or StatusNotifierItem), the manager shows this with a tray icon.
Windows always has a tray: the notification area of the taskbar.
Clicking the tray icon, with any button, opens the menu of the tray icon: Show manager (`Show`) and Quit manager (`Quit`).

On a desk with no tray, closing the manager window replaces the manager window with the 'tray icon window': a window of the size of a tray icon, with no title bar and no border, containing only the Fearless icon, in the bottom right corner of the screen and always above the other windows.
Clicking the 'tray icon window', with any button, opens the menu of the tray icon.
For half a second after that menu shows, a press or a release on the entries of that menu does nothing: the release of the click that opened the menu would otherwise choose the entry under the pointer.
An entry of that menu is chosen by a click: a press and a release of a button on that same entry.
`Show` removes the 'tray icon window' and shows the manager window.

Dropping files or folders on the manager window is a `Register` of each folder or `.fearless` file dropped, and an `OpenWithOrShortcut` of each other file dropped.
Every button is disabled for half a second after it is pressed.

A menu bar at the top, with two menus:
