## Run, Compile and Check

A 'mains chain' of a project runs the selected mains of one `Run`, one after the other, from the start of the first main to the end of the last one. An `OpenWithOrShortcut` running a main runs a 'mains chain' of that one main.
A project runs at most one thing at a time: a compilation or a 'mains chain'.
A 'compilation' of a project runs from the start of `Coordinator.compile` to the end of the last step of What the manager does after `Coordinator.compile`.
A project is 'busy' while a 'mains chain' or a compilation of the project runs, or a 'mains chain' of a project with a mount of the project runs.
Everything the mains of a 'mains chain' or a compilation print goes to the Output, and the Output marks where each main and the compilation start and end, and how each of them ended.

- The report of a `Check`, and the changes of a `Fix` (see Fix in FolderShapesDesign), go to the Output, and the Output has no marks for a `Check` or a `Fix`.
- A main that ends with an error, a crash included, ends the 'mains chain'.

### Files changing while a 'mains chain' runs
An 'autoCheck' that finds a project 'invalid', 'out of date' or unreachable ends the 'mains chain' of that project and every 'mains chain' of a project with a mount of that project; the Output of the project of each such 'mains chain' holds an error naming the project that changed and the main that was running.
```
A (greet.Diary->diary) in hello.mounts.
B The 'mains chain' of hello is running, at the main greet.Hello.
C The user renames the folder y2026 of diary to Y2026 with the file explorer.
D An 'autoCheck' of diary finds diary 'invalid'.
E The 'mains chain' of hello ends.
F The Output of hello holds an error naming diary and greet.Hello.
```

## File associations

### The extensions of the manager

```
M                  the manager; id(M) its identity; folder(M) its manager folder
D                  the desk (see Desks in FolderShapesDesign)
fearless           the 'Fearless extensions': .fearless, .fapp000 .. .fapp999, .ffile000 .. .ffile999; every other extension is a 'system extension'
reserve(M)         the 'reserve' of M: the list reserve of projects.info (see Registered projects)
cached(M)          the registered projects of M whose compilation cache contains a <pkg>.json
claims(p)          the extensions of the claims of Coordinator.cachedMains of p
captured(M)        the 'captured extensions' of M: fearless union reserve(M)
single(D)          the manager processes of D that are the 'single' manager
```

A `.reconcile` of M (see Reconciling) gives G, the extensions the user gives up (see ReconciliatorDesign); then:
```
T  G intersects fearless  =>  a 'fatal error' naming the Fearless extensions of G
   otherwise              =>  for each p in cached(M) with claims(p) intersecting G: the 'mains chain' and the compilation of p end,
                              and the compilation cache of p is deleted (Coordinator.deleteCache);
                              reserve(M) := (reserve(M) union claims(cached(M))) \ (fearless union G)
```
Only T and an `EditMetadata` change reserve(M).
After a `.reconcile` of M and T, while no other program changes D, R1 to R4 (see The data in ReconciliatorDesign) hold with i = id(M) and E = captured(M), and:
```
I1  forall p in cached(M). claims(p) subset of captured(M)
```
At every moment:
```
I2  |single(D)| <= 1
I3  M running && !exists(folder(M)) && M did not delete folder(M)  =>  M is applying Quit, and M changes nothing on D
```

The `replaced` of each `.reconcile` deletes the manager folder of the program of the command that `replaced` receives, when that command is inside the program folder of a manager (see Program and manager folder) and that manager folder is not the manager folder of the program calling `.reconcile`.
`replaced` deletes that manager folder only while holding the lock of the `instance.lock` of that folder (see The 'single' manager in Implementation notes).
When a manager process holds that lock, `replaced` writes a `Quit` message with a port into the manager folder, and waits for the lock:
- A 'single candidate' and the 'single' manager read a `Quit` message as soon as the `Quit` message arrives, also while applying another request.
- On the connection of the port, the manager process sends the identifier of its OS process, then, every 2 seconds until the manager process ends, a 'progress line': at most 80 characters saying what the manager process is doing and how much of it is done, with no time in it (`Ending the mains: 2 of 5 ended.`).
- When 10 seconds pass with no 'progress line' different from the 'progress line' before it, `replaced` ends that OS process.
The `Quit` message and the 'progress lines' are the same for every version of the manager.

